1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Ancient humans sometimes reproduced with close family members. But the shocking part is how rarely they seem to have done it. For generations, people

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
imagined prehistoric communities as tiny isolated bands trapped with the same relatives for thousands of years. If

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
only 20 people lived in your camp, the logic seems unavoidable. Eventually, everyone becomes someone's sibling,

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,000
cousin, aunt, uncle, or mysteriously familiar-looking grandfather. Then ancient DNA entered the discussion.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Researchers examined the genomes of 1,785 ancient individuals from different regions and periods. Only one carried

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
the genetic pattern expected from parents who were first-degree relatives, meaning a brother and sister, a parent and child, or an equivalent

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:52,000
relationship. One close cousin reproduction appeared more often, but even that was uncommon. The researchers

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:59,000
concluded that substantially fewer than 10% of the parental unions represented in their sample occurred between first

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,000
cousins or similarly close relatives. So yes, ancient humans reproduced with family members mostly. However, they

10
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:14,000
worked surprisingly hard not to. And that creates a better question. How did people living in small groups without family trees, birth certificates,

11
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:22,000
genetic testing, or an app politely announcing that their match shared 25% of their DNA? Avoid accidentally

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:32,000
reproducing with close relatives? The answer begins by correcting what an ancient community actually was. A camp was small. Its social world was not.

13
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Hunter gatherers might travel, sleep, hunt, and share food in groups containing only a few dozen people. But

14
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:47,000
those groups were rarely sealed biological containers. Individuals moved between camps. Bands separated and

15
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:54,000
reunited. Families visited distant allies. Seasonal gatherings brought several communities together. Materials

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:03,000
traveled across enormous distances carrying information, obligations, stories, and probably romantic opportunities with them. A person might

17
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:11,000
spend most days with 25 people while belonging to a social network containing hundreds. This distinction matters because population size is not the same

18
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:18,000
as camp size. If 20 people became permanently isolated and reproduced only among themselves, relatedness would rise

19
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:26,000
rapidly. But if even a few individuals moved between that camp and neighboring groups each generation, they could

20
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:35,000
introduce enough genetic diversity to prevent the community from becoming one enormous family reunion with increasingly complicated consequences.

21
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Ancient humans did not need to understand genetics. They needed visitors. And the evidence suggests they had them. Stone tools made from distant

22
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:53,000
raw materials appear far from their geological sources. Shells traveled inland. Pigments, ornaments, styles, and

23
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:02,000
technologies moved between groups. These objects may have passed through several hands, so they do not prove that every owner personally completed an impressive

24
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:10,000
prehistoric road trip. But they reveal networks extending beyond individual camps. People exchanged more than rocks.

25
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:18,000
They exchanged knowledge, hospitality, food, obligations, and partners. Among many historically observed

26
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:25,000
huntergatherer societies, marriage links different groups. A person leaves the community where they were raised and

27
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:34,000
joins another or couples move flexibly between relatives. Which sex moves varies. Some societies are more patrolal

28
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:43,000
with women moving toward the husband's group. Others are matral, flexible or change according to resources and relationships.

29
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:50,000
The important mechanism is movement. One group's daughter becomes another group's mother. Her children grow up surrounded

30
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:59,000
by their father's relatives but carry ancestry from somewhere else. In another society, a man moves to live near his wife's family and performs the same

31
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:08,000
genetic service while presumably being evaluated by several highly interested in-laws. This was not necessarily a conscious anti-inbreeding program.

32
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Marriage could create alliances, settle disputes, grant access to territory, redistribute labor, or connect people

33
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:23,000
during shortages. A partner from another community brought knowledge of different routes, plants, hunting grounds, languages, and relatives willing to

34
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
help. Genetic diversity was a side effect of being socially connected.

35
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Ancient DNA has begun revealing these movements. At Chagirkaya Cave in southern Siberia, researchers recovered DNA from a remarkable group of

36
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Neanderthalss who lived roughly 54,000 years ago. The individuals included a father and his adolescent daughter along with other biological relationships.

37
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Their genomes showed very low diversity.

38
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:57,000
These Neanderthalss belonged to a small population and their community may have contained only around 20 individuals. If any humans appeared designed for

39
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:06,000
unavoidable family reproduction, this group would seem to qualify. But the DNA contained another clue. The diversity in

40
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:12,000
mitochondrial DNA inherited through mothers was much greater than the diversity in Y chromosomes inherited

41
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:21,000
through paternal lines. The best fitting explanation was that many women moved into the community from other Neanderthal groups, while men more often

42
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:29,000
remained where they were born. Even Neanderthalss living near the edge of their known range apparently had connections beyond one cave. Their

43
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:38,000
community was small. Their mating pool was larger. This does not mean Neanderthalss never reproduced with relatives. Their genomes contain many

44
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:45,000
short stretches of identical DNA, indicating long-term small population sizes and background relatedness. Some

45
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:54,000
individuals show that their recent ancestors also came from limited populations. But there is a crucial difference between everyone being distantly related and someone

46
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:03,000
reproducing with a sibling. A population can carry low genetic diversity because its members descend from a small ancestral population, experienced

47
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:11,000
bottlenecks, or repeatedly partnered within a limited regional network that produces many shorter runs of homozygosity.

48
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Stretches where both chromosome copies are identical. A recent close relative union produces fewer but much longer

49
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:25,000
stretches. Imagine taking two copies of an enormous book. Distant relatives inherited scattered matching sentences

50
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:34,000
from ancestors far back in time. A child of siblings receives entire matching chapters because the parents inherited large overlapping pieces from the same

51
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:39,000
recent parents. Geneticists call these matching sections runs of homozygosity.

52
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:48,000
They are how dead people reveal relationships they never wrote down. The biological danger comes from recessive varants. Every human carries potentially

53
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:56,000
harmful mutations. Usually, a functioning copy inherited from the other parent prevents a recessive mutation from causing disease. Unrelated

54
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:05,000
parents are less likely to carry the same rare harmful variant. Close relatives inherited DNA from many of the same ancestors. If two siblings

55
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:13,000
reproduce, their child has a much greater chance of receiving identical harmful varants from both sides. The risk of congenital disorders, reduced

56
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:21,000
fertility, developmental problems, weakened immunity, still birth and infant death can rise. This is called

57
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:29,000
inbreeding depression. It is real. It is also frequently portrayed badly. A child born to related parents does not

58
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:37,000
automatically emerge as a deformed creature who immediately confirms every medieval painting. Many such children appear healthy. The danger is

59
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:44,000
statistical, not guaranteed. One close relative union might produce surviving children without obvious disorders.

60
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:53,000
Repeated close inbreeding across generations allows harmful recessive varants to meet more often and makes the consequences harder to escape. Evolution

61
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:57,000
does not punish the family because it finds the relationship morally awkward.

62
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:04,000
It exposes matching genetic vulnerabilities. Ancient people could observe those consequences without understanding chromosomes. If unions

63
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:12,000
between certain relatives repeatedly produced fewer surviving children, communities might connect the relationship with danger. But biological

64
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:20,000
observation was probably only part of the story. Humans also seem to possess psychological mechanisms discouraging attraction toward people raised as close

65
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:26,000
childhood companions. This is commonly associated with the western mark effect.

66
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:33,000
The basic idea is that close domestic association during early childhood reduces later sexual attraction. The brain does not need to calculate genetic

67
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:43,000
relatedness. It uses childhood familiarity as a rough clue. That clue is imperfect. Biological siblings raised apart may not develop the same aversion.

68
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:52,000
Unrelated children raised closely together may treat one another psychologically like siblings. Human kin recognition also uses other information,

69
00:08:52,000 --> 00:09:00,000
including seeing one's mother care for a newborn, family testimony, resemblance, names, and social roles. Still,

70
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:09,000
childhood co-residents gives the mind a simple rule. The person stealing your food every morning throughout childhood is probably not your ideal reproductive

71
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:17,000
partner. Studies in several cultural settings support this effect. People raised together from early childhood tend to show reduced attraction and

72
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:20,000
stronger opposition to sexual relationships between close relatives.

73
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Historical examples involving communal child rearing and arranged minor marriages also suggest that intense early familiarity can suppress later sexual interest.

74
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:37,000
Nature did not install an infallible incest detector. It installed an extremely aggressive sibling vibe.

75
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:43,000
Culture strengthened it. Human societies classify kin obsessively.

76
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:51,000
Languages contain words distinguishing mother from aunt, older brother from younger brother, parallel cousin from cross cousin, biological parent from

77
00:09:51,000 --> 00:10:00,000
social parent, and relatives connected through marriage. Some systems make the average modern family tree look like a children's menu. These categories

78
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:07,000
regulate responsibility. They determine who shares food, inherits property, protects children, avenges injury,

79
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:14,000
performs rituals, or can become a partner. A person may be biologically distant yet classified as a forbidden

80
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:22,000
sibling. Another may be a genetic cousin but belong to a socially preferred marriage category. By the way, if you

81
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:29,000
want to go even deeper into the secret lives of ancient humans, I just launched a full course called The Secret Lives of

82
