Ancient Humans. 10 modules, 3 plus hours of content covering everything we never talk about on YouTube. what they feared,

83
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:46,000
how they communicated, what hunted them, and much more, plus an exclusive PDF guide included. Link in the description.

84
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:56,000
See you inside. This is why did ancient humans reproduce with family? Cannot be answered using biology alone. Family is

85
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:04,000
both genetic and social. Cousin marriage illustrates the problem. To many modern viewers, the word cousin sounds immediately close, but there are

86
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:11,000
degrees. First cousins share grandparents. Second cousins share great-grandparents.

87
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:19,000
Beyond that, the biological relationship decreases quickly. In a small regional population, almost every potential partner may be a distant cousin if the

88
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:28,000
genealogy is followed far enough. At some point, reproducing with relatives becomes another way of saying reproducing with humans from the same

89
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:37,000
population. Ancient people did not need partners with zero shared ancestry. that would eventually require leaving the species. They needed to avoid

90
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:45,000
excessively close ancestry. Some societies prohibited all recognized cousins. Others encouraged marriage between particular cousins because it

91
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:53,000
reinforced alliances, kept property within a lineage, or renewed relationships between families. Cross cousins, the children of a parents

92
00:11:53,000 --> 00:12:03,000
opposite sex sibling, were preferred partners in some kinship systems, while parallel cousins could be treated as siblings and forbidden. To an outsider,

93
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:10,000
both are first cousins. Socially, they may belong to opposite categories. These rules could circulate partners

94
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:22,000
systematically between groups. If one lineage regularly gave spouses to another and received spouses in return, marriage became more than individual attraction. It became infrastructure.

95
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:32,000
The exchange carried genes, but also trust. Your partner's relatives were people you could visit, trade with, or call upon during conflict.

96
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:39,000
Children connected two groups that might otherwise compete. In dangerous environments, an extended network was insurance that happened to contain a

97
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:46,000
wedding. That is likely one reason extreme incest remained rare, even when people lived in small communities.

98
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:53,000
Avoidance was socially useful before anyone noticed the genetics. Then settlements grew. Agriculture is often

99
00:12:53,000 --> 00:13:00,000
assumed to have solved the problem by placing more people nearby. In some ways, it did. Larger populations offered

100
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:10,000
more potential partners. Early villages could receive newcomers, trade with neighbors, and support wider regional networks. But settlement also created

101
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:19,000
new reasons to control marriage. Land, livestock, stored grain, houses, and inherited status could be kept within

102
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:26,000
families. Lineages became politically important. Some communities practiced endogamy, choosing partners from within

103
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:33,000
a defined group. Others became strongly petrilical with women moving between male- centered households. Ancient

104
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:41,000
genomes from Neolithic and Bronze Age cemeteries frequently reveal local groups of related men accompanied by women with more diverse ancestry. At

105
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:52,000
Gurgy and France, researchers reconstructed enormous pedigrees spanning up to seven generations. The community was patrineal and prolo while women generally came from other groups.

106
00:13:52,000 --> 00:14:01,000
This arrangement kept sons near fathers while importing partners. It concentrated property without completely concentrating the genome. Other

107
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:08,000
communities organized themselves differently. The first farming settlements were not running one universal marriage operating system.

108
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:16,000
Some households contained few close biological relatives, showing that living together did not always mean belonging to one nuclear family.

109
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:25,000
Adoption, fostering, remarage, friendship, servitude, and ritual relationships could shape a household alongside ancestry. A house was not

110
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:30,000
automatically a genetic box. A grave was not automatically a family photo.

111
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:37,000
Ancient DNA has made that distinction painfully important. People buried together may not be close relatives. Close relatives may be buried far apart.

112
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:46,000
A prestigious tomb might represent lineage, office, ritual membership or political identity rather than everyone who shared dinner. Then there are the

113
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:53,000
exceptions. One of the most disturbing comes from New Graange in Ireland. New Graange is an enormous passage tomb

114
00:14:53,000 --> 00:15:00,000
built more than 5,000 years ago. Its construction required coordinated labor on a massive scale. Deep inside, in the

115
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:08,000
most prestigious part of the monument, researchers recovered the remains of an adult man. His genome contained extraordinary amounts of long identical

116
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:16,000
DNA. His parents were firstderee relatives. The possible relationships include brother and sister or parent and

117
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:24,000
child. Genetics alone cannot distinguish them reliably because both produce similar levels of shared ancestry. The

118
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:27,000
location makes the discovery even stranger.

119
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:36,000
This was not an anonymous individual discarded outside the settlement. He was placed within a monument associated with extraordinary ritual power. Researchers

120
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:44,000
suggested that his parentage may reflect a socially sanctioned union within a ruling elite. Perhaps a dynasty maintaining sacred status through

121
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:52,000
extreme endogamy. That interpretation is plausible. It is not proven. The genome tells us his parents were exceptionally

122
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:58,000
close relatives. The monument tells us he received unusual treatment. It cannot tell us whether the union was

123
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:08,000
consensual, coerced, secret, sacred, or simply survived despite violating a rule. Prehistory preserved the scandal.

124
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:15,000
It deleted the testimony. A newer case from Bronze Age southern Italy is even more genetically specific. At Grata

125
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:23,000
Delam Monica, researchers analyzed a young male with nearly 800 centmorggans of long homozygous segments, an extreme

126
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:32,000
result indicating firstderee parental relatedness. The researchers also identified his father among the buried individuals. Genetic markers showed that

127
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:41,000
the father was not connected to the child's mother through her maternal line. Pedigree reconstruction produced a grim conclusion. The boy's father also

128
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:49,000
appears to have been the mother's father. The child was born from a father-daughter union. Again, the DNA cannot reveal the circumstances. It does

129
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:55,000
not tell us whether this was abuse, a socially permitted practice, a dynastic arrangement, or an isolated act.

130
00:16:55,000 --> 00:17:04,000
Considering the power imbalance inherent in the relationship, inventing a romantic explanation would be especially irresponsible. The boy's genome records

131
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:12,000
reproduction. It does not record consent. Other extreme cases have appeared in Neolithic or calcolithic remains from Israel and Malta. They

132
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:22,000
remain extraordinarily rare compared with the thousands of ancient individuals now studied. That rarity matters. An unusual burial can attract

133
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:29,000
enormous attention and distort the entire past. Ancient elite born from incest becomes memorable. Most people

134
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:36,000
found partners who were not immediate relatives does not arrive wearing enough archaeological drama. But normality is

135
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:47,000
the larger discovery. The broad ancient DNA survey found only one firstderee case among 1,785 individuals in its own data set.

136
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:56,000
54 showed long homozygous segments consistent with close parental relatedness, often around first cousin level. Even that 3% figure is an upper

137
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:04,000
estimate for actual cousin unions because small mating populations can generate similar patterns without the parents being known first cousins. These

138
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:11,000
samples are not a perfect census of humanity. Ancient DNA preserves unevenly. Cold environments and certain

139
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:18,000
bones preserve it better. Europe and parts of Eurasia are heavily represented while tropical regions remain under

140
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:25,000
sampled. Burial customs determine whose remains survive. Elites may receive better preserved tombs. Cremation

141
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:31,000
destroys DNA. Infants who died without formal burial may disappear entirely.

142
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:38,000
There is another uncomfortable possibility. Children born from extreme inbreeding might have died before receiving the kinds of burials

143
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:47,000
researchers sample. Some societies may have killed infants produced by forbidden unions. Others may have concealed the event. Ancient DNA can

144
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:54,000
only analyze the people archaeology recovers. 1 in 1,785 does not mean exactly one firstderee

145
00:18:54,000 --> 00:19:02,000
union occurred for every 1,785 births. It means such unions were very rare among the sampled individuals who

146
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:09,000
left usable genomes. The pattern is still overwhelming. Ancient populations were not casually ignoring close

147
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:17,000
kinship. Even communities with high background relatedness usually avoided immediate relatives. Why then did some

148
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:24,000
elites do the opposite? Power changes the rules. In ancient Egypt, royal sibling marriage became famous because

149
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:33,000
certain dynasties used it to maintain divine identity and political legitimacy. Gods married siblings in Egyptian mythology. A king and queen

150
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:38,000
could present themselves as sacred counterparts while keeping royal status within the dynasty.

151
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:48,000
The Tamies later embraced sibling marriage with enough enthusiasm to make their family tree resemble a recycling symbol. Cleopatra IIIth's ancestry

152
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:58,000
included repeated close-kin unions. The dynasty was Macedonian Greek in origin, but adopted and intensified traditions that supported royal exclusivity.

153
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:07,000
Other ruling systems, including some elites in ancient or pre-colonial Hawaii and the Inca world, also permitted exceptionally close unions to preserve

154
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:15,000
sacred rank. These practices were memorable because ordinary rules did not apply to rulers. If everyone routinely

155
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:23,000
married siblings, royal incest would not demonstrate divine difference. Its political value came partly from crossing a boundary that structured

156
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:32,000
normal life. The ruler was so sacred that even the incest taboo could be converted into a privilege. Or more realistically, power could force

157
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:37,000
everyone else to call it sacred. Elite families also had material advantages.

158
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:46,000
They could support children with health problems, secure multiple partners if fertility declined, command wet nurses and servants, and conceal consequences

159
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:54,000
behind palace walls. A struggling forager family could not convert reduced fertility into a ceremonial success.

160
00:20:54,000 --> 00:21:01,000
Still, dynastic inbreeding carried costs. Repeated close unions increased the chance that harmful varants would

161
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:06,000
meet. Some royal lineages developed serious health and fertility problems.

162
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:17,000
Yet connecting a specific ancient ruler's appearance or illness directly to incest requires caution. Portraits were stylized. Mummies are incomplete.

163
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:26,000
Diagnoses performed across thousands of years have a remarkable tendency to become confident exactly where evidence becomes weakest. Genetics explains

164
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:33,000
increased risk. It does not turn every unusual jaw into a solved case. For ordinary ancient people, preventing

165
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:38,000
close family reproduction probably did not feel like one organized strategy.

166
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:47,000
Several defenses overlapped. Children developed aversion toward those raised beside them. Adults taught kin categories. Communities prohibited

167
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:54,000
certain relationships. One sex or both moved between groups. Seasonal gatherings introduced new people.

168
00:21:54,000 --> 00:22:01,000
Marriage built alliances across camps or villages. Language preserved ancestry beyond what appearance could reveal.

169
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:10,000
Gossip handled the remaining quality control. A person considering a partner did not need to remember seven generations. Parents, grandparents, and

170
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:18,000
elders carried social memory. They knew who had arrived from where, whose child someone was, and which union would

171
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:28,000
create a dispute. Oral societies were not societies without records. The records were alive and occasionally judgmental. These systems could fail.

172
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:34,000
Parents could be misidentified. People could be separated and later reunited.

173
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:42,000
Violence and coercion could override rules. Population crashes might leave few potential partners. Islands, remote

174
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:49,000
valleys, disasters, warfare, or epidemic loss could narrow the mating network. In

175
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:55,000
those conditions, people faced a choice between related reproduction and no reproduction.

176
00:22:55,000 --> 00:23:03,000
Some chose the relative. This was not necessarily irrational. The genetic risk from one cousin union may be smaller than the demographic risk of a community

177
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:10,000
producing no children. A tiny population cannot preserve theoretical genetic purity while going extinct with

178
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:15,000
excellent principles. Human groups needed balance.

179
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:24,000
Too little connection beyond the community increased inbreeding. Too much permanent separation weakened cooperation inside it. Our unusual

180
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:31,000
social structure solved both problems by building nested circles. Household, camp, lineage, neighboring band,

181
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:39,000
seasonal gathering, trading network, and wider cultural population. People lived with a manageable number of companions while maintaining access to partners and

182
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:47,000
allies elsewhere. That may have been one of humanity's greatest survival advantages. Neanderthalss also moved

183
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:55,000
between communities, but their total populations remained small and fragmented for long periods. Modern humans arriving in Eurasia may have

184
00:23:55,000 --> 00:24:03,000
maintained broader or denser social networks, although the evidence remains debated and varied across time and place. Wider connections could preserve

185
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:10,000
genetic diversity, spread innovations, and provide support when one local group failed. A distant partner was therefore

186
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:16,000
not merely a romantic possibility. They were population maintenance.

187
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:23,000
They carried unfamiliar immune variants, new relatives, different skills, and proof that the world continued beyond

188
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:30,000
the next ridge. This makes the original question sound almost backward. Ancient humans did not reproduce with relatives

189
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:37,000
simply because they lived in small groups. They repeatedly reorganized those groups so they would not have to.

190
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:44,000
The camp expanded through marriage. The village recruited outsiders. Young adults moved. Families exchanged

191
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:48,000
partners. Gatherings renewed relationships.

192
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:56,000
Taboss turned biological danger into social prohibition long before anyone knew what a recessive alil was. When

193
00:24:56,000 --> 00:25:04,000
those mechanisms failed, or when rulers deliberately broke them, DNA preserved the result. A man beneath New Graange

194
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:12,000
carries enormous matching sections inherited from firstdegree relatives. A boy in an Italian cave reveals that his father was also his maternal

195
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:21,000
grandfather. Neanderthalss in Siberia reveal deep isolation, but also women arriving from neighboring communities.

196
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:33,000
Thousands of other ancient people reveal something less sensational and more important. Their parents were not extremely close kin. So, did ancient humans reproduce with family members?

197
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:36,000
Yes, cousins sometimes partnered.

198
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:44,000
Isolated populations carried substantial shared ancestry. A few individuals were born from sibling or parent child

199
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:52,000
unions. Certain elites transformed close-kin marriage into political theater. Coercion, crisis, and limited

200
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:59,000
choice almost certainly produced cases archaeology will never recover. But the image of prehistoric humans trapped in

201
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:06,000
one cave and eventually selecting whichever sibling looked least annoyed is mostly wrong. Ancient communities

202
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:14,000
were mobile, connected, and socially intelligent. They recognized kin, regulated relationships, exchanged

203
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:22,000
partners, and maintained networks much larger than the camp itself. They did not understand DNA.

204
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:31,000
They understood that some people were family. They understood that partners often needed to come from somewhere else. And when another group appeared on

205
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:39,000
the horizon, bringing unfamiliar faces, tools, stories, and accents, the encounter offered more than trade. It

206
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:42,000
kept humanity's family tree from becoming a circle.
